{"authored" [{"question" "q-113750" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=12x+9{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3}$ have a local maximum?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-113750" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =12+18x-12{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-6(2{x}^{2}-3x-2) \\\\  & =-6(x-2)(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-6(x-2)(2x+1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2},2.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,0,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-6(-1-2)(2(-1)+1)=-18<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =-6(0-2)(2(0)+1)=12>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =-6(3-2)(2(3)+1)=-42<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2})$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $0$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=2$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local maximum at $x=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-113750" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b519fcd567150762be2acc0f209e3599855b11460e4d739b98ee1f3be3f4b4fa" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-113750" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-113750" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-113750" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-113750" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-113750" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-113750" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$f(x)$ does not have any local maxima"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-113758" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-\\frac{5}{3}$ is a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is a local minimum\n- $x=\\frac{5}{3}$ is a local minimum" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-113758" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}+10x \\\\  & =2x(3x+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(3x+5)\\,⟹\\,x=0,-\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0$ and $x=-\\frac{5}{3}.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =2(-2)(3(-2)+5)=4>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =2(-1)(3(-1)+5)=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =2(1)(3(1)+5)=16>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{3})$ | $-\\frac{5}{3}$ | $(-\\frac{5}{3},0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\frac{5}{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{5}{3}$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local minimum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/r