egin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}=(x+2)x{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)x{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (x+2)x & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=-2$ and $x=0$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-3,-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-3) & =(-3+2)(-3){e}^{-3}=3{e}^{-3}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =(-1+2)(-1){e}^{-1}=-{e}^{-1}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =(1+2)(1){e}^{1}=3e>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-3$ |  | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-2$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49022" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b519fcd567150762be2acc0f209e3599855b11460e4d739b98ee1f3be3f4b4fa" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49022" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49022" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49022" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49022" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49022" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value"