"question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3}=2{x}^{2}(3-2x).\n$$\n\nAnd now find the stationary points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}(3-2x)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =2(-1)^{2}(3-2(-1))=10>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =2(1)^{2}(3-2(1))=2>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =2(2)^{2}(3-2(2))=-8<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79001" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b519fcd567150762be2acc0f209e3599855b11460e4d739b98ee1f3be3f4b4fa" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79001" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79001" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79001" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79001" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79001" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79001" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79001" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+12x-5,$ which of the following statements are true?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-12x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-12x+12 & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-4x+4) & =0 \\\\ 3(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=2$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=0,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =3(0)^{2}-12(0)+12=12>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =3(3)^{2}-12(3)+12=3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=2.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=2$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b519fcd567150762be2acc0f209e3599855b11460e4d739b98ee1f3be3f4b4fa" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "There is a local maximum at $x=0$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "There is a local maximum at $x=2$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "There is a local minimum at $x=0$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "There is a local minimum at $x=2$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the local maxima and minima for $f(x) = 6x-x^3-5,$ and classify each point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/example-3727.json" "file_sha256" "b14506a4c338bfe3e67ec003fceef852f02354db8bb254ced8de9d74f3c96c24" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 6-3x^2.\n$$\n\nSolving $f'(x) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n3(2-x^2)=0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=\\pm \\sqrt 2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -2, 0, 2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =6-3(-2)^{2}=-6 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =6-3(0)^{2}=6 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =6-3(2)^{2}=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty,-\\sqrt{2}\\right)$ | $-\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\left(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}\\right)$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\left(\\sqrt{2},\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{blue}-$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ | $0$ | $\\color{blue}-$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ | min | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ | max | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\sqrt{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-\\sqrt{2}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=\\sqrt{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\sqrt{2}$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local minimum at $x = -\\sqrt 2$ and one local maximum at $x = \\sqrt 2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/example-3727.json" "file_sha256" "b14506a4c338bfe3e67ec003fceef852f02354db8bb254ced8de9d74f3c96c24" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the relative maximum and minimum points of the function $f(x) = (2x+3)e^x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/example-3730.json" "file_sha256" "6dac08692757d9d4c92988a585c59f282242ded62cc37de5cae74a1f27af0c36" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(2x+3){e}^{x}+2{e}^{x}=(2x+5){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $f'(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+5){e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ 2x+5 & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x = -\\dfrac{5}{2}$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -5,0$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-5) & =(2(-5)+5){e}^{-5}=-\\frac{5}{{e}^{5}}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =(2(0)+5){e}^{0}=5>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty, -\\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$ | $-\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $\\left( -\\dfrac{5}{2},\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-5$ |  | $0$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{blue}-$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ | min | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\nAt $x=-\\dfrac{5}{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive.\n\nSo, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x = -\\dfrac{5}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/example-3730.json" "file_sha256" "6dac08692757d9d4c92988a585c59f282242ded62cc37de5cae74a1f27af0c36" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the stationary points of the function $f(x) =x^3$ and classify each point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/example-3729.json" "file_sha256" "9b4f7d8cb9c570e71ecedda4ba4aed81425ebc4f1cc8825d9754ab191f5aa95c" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) \t=3x^2,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n3x^2 = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=0.\n$$\n\nSo $x = 0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =3(-1)^{2}=3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =3(1)^{2}=3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty, 0\\right)$ | $0$ | $\\left(0,\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{red}+$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |  | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nSo, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/example-3729.json" "file_sha256" "9b4f7d8cb9c570e71ecedda4ba4aed81425ebc4f1cc8825d9754ab191f5aa95c" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-78983" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-3x+5$ have a local minimum?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "12231439f281f95060a790f14c889be058c9d0bbc12b480af06d797e1bd23a0e"} {"question" "q-78983" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-3 \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n3({x}^{2}-1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=\\pm 1.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =3((-2)^{2}-1)=9>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3((0)^{2}-1)=-3<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =3((2)^{2}-1)=9>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-1,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-1$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local minimum at $x=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "12231439f281f95060a790f14c889be058c9d0bbc12b480af06d797e1bd23a0e"} {"question" "q-78983" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "75475ea4b7463cabcd8545f73218469ca3eaf032b72cf1e124f044def2dbf532"} {"question" "q-78983" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "12231439f281f95060a790f14c889be058c9d0bbc12b480af06d797e1bd23a0e"} {"question" "q-78983" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "5"] ["text" "$f(x)$ does not have any local minima"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "12231439f281f95060a790f14c889be058c9d0bbc12b480af06d797e1bd23a0e"} {"question" "q-78983" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "12231439f281f95060a790f14c889be058c9d0bbc12b480af06d797e1bd23a0e"} {"question" "q-113758" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-\\frac{5}{3}$ is a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is a local minimum\n- $x=\\frac{5}{3}$ is a local minimum" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "12231439f281f95060a790f14c889be058c9d0bbc12b480af06d797e1bd23a0e"} {"question" "q-113758" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}+10x \\\\  & =2x(3x+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(3x+5)\\,⟹\\,x=0,-\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0$ and $x=-\\frac{5}{3}.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =2(-2)(3(-2)+5)=4>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =2(-1)(3(-1)+5)=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =2(1)(3(1)+5)=16>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{3})$ | $-\\frac{5}{3}$ | $(-\\frac{5}{3},0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\frac{5}{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{5}{3}$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local minimum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "12231439f281f95060a790f14c889be058c9d0bbc12b480af06d797e1bd23a0e"} {"question" "q-113758" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/activity-metadata.