3$ and $x=1.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-4,0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-4) & =3(-4)^{2}+6(-4)-9=15>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3(0)^{2}+6(0)-9=-9<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =3(2)^{2}+6(2)-9=15>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-4$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-3,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-3$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local minimum at $x=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "12231439f281f95060a790f14c889be058c9d0bbc12b480af06d797e1bd23a0e"} {"question" "q-344272" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "75475ea4b7463cabcd8545f73218469ca3eaf032b72cf1e124f044def2dbf532"} {"question" "q-344272" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "12231439f281f95060a790f14c889be058c9d0bbc12b480af06d797e1bd23a0e"} {"question" "q-344272" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "12231439f281f95060a790f14c889be058c9d0bbc12b480af06d797e1bd23a0e"} {"question" "q-21467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-12x+6,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-2$ is a local maximum\n- $x=1$ is a local minimum\n- $x=1$ is a local maximum" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "12231439f281f95060a790f14c889be058c9d0bbc12b480af06d797e1bd23a0e"} {"question" "q-21467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}+6x-12 \\\\  & =6({x}^{2}+x-2) \\\\  & =6(x+2)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(x+2)(x-1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-2$ and $x=1.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-3,0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-3) & =6(-3+2)(-3-1)=24>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =6(0+2)(0-1)=-12<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =6(2+2)(2-1)=24>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-3$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-2$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local minimum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "12231439f281f95060a790f14c889be058c9d0bbc12b480af06d797e1bd23a0e"} {"question" "q-21467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "75475ea4b7463cabcd8545f73218469ca3eaf032b72cf1e124f044def2dbf532"} {"question" "q-21467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "12231439f281f95060a790f14c889be058c9d0bbc12b480af06d797e1bd23a0e"} {"question" "q-21467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "12231439f281f95060a790f14c889be058c9d0bbc12b480af06d797e1bd23a0e"} {"question" "q-21467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "12231439f281f95060a790f14c889be058c9d0bbc12b480af06d797e1bd23a0e"} {"question" "q-113750" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=12x+9{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3}$ have a local maximum?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "12231439f281f95060a790f14c889be058c9d0bbc12b480af06d797e1bd23a0e"} {"question" "q-113750" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =12+18x-12{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-6(2{x}^{2}-3x-2) \\\\  & =-6(x-2)(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-6(x-2)(2x+1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2},2.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,0,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-6(-1-2)(2(-1)+1)=-18<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =-6(0-2)(2(0)+1)=12>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =-6(3-2)(2(3)+1)=-42<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2})$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $0$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=2$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local maximum at $x=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "12231439f281f95060a790f14c889be058c9d0bbc12b480af06d797e1bd23a0e"} {"question" "q-113750" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "75475ea4b7463cabcd8545f73218469ca3eaf032b72cf1e124f044def2dbf532"} {"question" "q-113750" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "12231439f281f95060a790f14c889be058c9d0bbc12b480af06d797e1bd23a0e"} {"question" "q-113750" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["text" "$f(x)$ does not have any local maxima"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "12231439f281f95060a790f14c889be058c9d0bbc12b480af06d797e1bd23a0e"} {"question" "q-113750" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "12231439f281f95060a790f14c889be058c9d0bbc12b480af06d797e1bd23a0e"} {"question" "q-22504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ have a local maximum?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "12231439f281f95060a790f14c889be058c9d0bbc12b480af06d797e1bd23a0e"} {"question" "q-22504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot {x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot 2x}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ on $(0,\\infty )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{3}} & =0 \\\\ 1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & ={e}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x={e}^{1/2}$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x={e}^{1/3},e$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}({e}^{1/3}) & =\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{1/3}}{{({e}^{1/3})}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{3e}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(e) & =\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e}{{e}^{3}}=-\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,{e}^{1/2})$ | ${e}^{1/2}$ | $({e}^{1/2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | ${e}^{1/3}$ |  | $e$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x={e}^{1/2},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x={e}^{1/2}$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x={e}^{1/2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "12231439f281f95060a790f14c889be058c9d0bbc12b480af06d797e1bd23a0e"} {"question" "q-22504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/