 is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-(-1){e}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{e}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-(1){e}^{1}=-e<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "12231439f281f95060a790f14c889be058c9d0bbc12b480af06d797e1bd23a0e"} {"question" "q-49033" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "75475ea4b7463cabcd8545f73218469ca3eaf032b72cf1e124f044def2dbf532"} {"question" "q-49033" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "12231439f281f95060a790f14c889be058c9d0bbc12b480af06d797e1bd23a0e"} {"question" "q-49033" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["text" "$f(x)$ does not have any local maxima"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/